<!--Q#7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Explain your reasoning for the solution. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para><b>Answer:</b></para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>Isolate the variable \(m\).</para>
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}8c+m&amp;=&amp;178.50\\m&amp;=&amp;178.50-8c\end{array}\)</para>
    <para>Substitute</para>
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}20c+10(178.50-8c)&amp;=&amp;495\\20c+1785-80c&amp;=&amp;495\\-60c+1785&amp;=&amp;495\\-60c&amp;=&amp;-1290\\c&amp;=&amp;21.50\end{array}\)</para>
    
    <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}m&amp;=&amp;178.50-8(21.50)\\m&amp;=&amp;178.50-172\\m&amp;=&amp;6.50\end{array}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>Use the following information to answer questions 8 – 11:</para>
<para>To be reimbursed for the cost of the supplies, the teacher recorded, “Items purchased: 28 calculators and 11 measuring tapes. Amount: $673.50.”</para>

<!--Q#8-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write an equation to represent the relationship between the numbers of calculators and measuring tapes, the prices of those supplies, and the total amount spent. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para><b>Answer:</b></para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. \(28c+11m=673.50\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#8-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How is this equation related to the first two equations? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para><b>Answer:</b></para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. It is the sum of the first two equations.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#9-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> In this situation, what do the solutions of this equation mean? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para><b>Answer:</b></para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The solutions are all possible combinations of prices for a calculator and for a measuring tape that make a total of $673.50 when 28 calculators and 11 measuring tapes are bought.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#11-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How many possible solutions does this equation have? How many solutions make sense in this situation? Be prepared to show your reasoning. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. It has many possible solutions, but only one solution makes sense. The prices of a calculator and of a measuring tape in this equation are the same as those in the other two equations. The new equation simply shows the total numbers of supplies and the total amount paid.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
